\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pathfinder-note}

\title{Correcting a Resource Mechanism Before Importing CVaR Learning}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{The pair and the connexion pursued}

Q proposes quadratic payments for strategic resource allocation,
claiming efficient Nash implementation, budget balance, individual
rationality, and stochastic learning stability.
P develops distributed zeroth-order CVaR optimization and identifies
the expected empirical smoothed objective targeted by its
fixed-batch method.

The connexion pursued was to replace Q's expected valuations with
risk-averse preferences learned through P's empirical CVaR oracle.
The investigation changed direction when preliminary checks
invalidated Q's mechanism guarantees: a payment correction and a
different convergence argument were needed before transferring
P's oracle.
\ledger{1, 3, 6, 12}

The preceding account established mechanism counterexamples,
a corrected payment, surrogate economic guarantees, regularized
learning, multilevel estimation, a private-threshold oracle,
an atom-safe Lipschitz-bias bound, and a saddle-gap transfer theorem.
These results are retained below.
Reviews \ledger{14, 27, 37} report independent rederivations of
successive portions without finding an error.
The latest review, \ledger{37}, nevertheless questioned the
oracle access model, the batch baseline, and the use of allocation
curvature in the query bound.

The appended round answers those questions with independent
symmetric batches, locally feasible queries, and a weighted
block-learning argument.
Its strongest conditional upper bound is
\(O(\epsilon^{-3})\) total loss evaluations for either private
thresholds or symmetric empirical batches.
It supersedes any interpretation of the earlier comparison as a
threshold query-exponent advantage.
These newest arguments have exact algebra checks but no subsequent
independent verifier review in the supplied ledger.
\ledger{38, 39, 40, 42, 43, 45, 46}

The principal accounts are
\url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md},
\url{ada/finite_cost_extension.md},
\url{ada/private_threshold_oracle.md},
\url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, and
\url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}.
The results concern prescribed learning and economic properties
of its outputs.
Publication priority, optimal oracle complexity, and incentives
to follow the transient protocol remain unresolved.

\section{Obstructions in Q}

\begin{claim}
Q's strict uniqueness LMI and its price-consensus implementation
condition cannot hold simultaneously.
\end{claim}

Q requires
\[
\Psi+\Psi^\top\preceq-\upsilon I,\qquad
\upsilon>0,\qquad
\sum_m A_{nm}^n=0.
\]
For a nonzero \(v\in\mathbb R^K\), take \(h_n=(0,v)\).
Then
\[
h^\top(\Psi+\Psi^\top)h
=-2\sum_n v^\top
 \left(\sum_m A_{nm}^n\right)v=0.
\]
This contradicts strict negative definiteness, independently of
valuations or CVaR.
It disproves simultaneous LMI feasibility, without proving
inefficiency of every equilibrium of the explicit payment.
\ledger{2, 14, 37}

\begin{objection}
Failure of a sufficient LMI does not itself disprove individual
rationality.
\end{objection}

For Q's explicit payment,
\[
\sum_m a_m^n=\frac{\alpha c}{N(N-1)},
\]
contradicting its nonpositivity condition when a capacity is
positive.
A direct counterexample is stronger.
Take
\[
\begin{gathered}
N=3,\quad K=1,\quad \alpha=c=1,\quad
\mathcal X_n=[0,2],\\
V_1(x)=5x-\frac{x^2}{2},\qquad
V_2(x)=V_3(x)=-x-\frac{x^2}{2}.
\end{gathered}
\]
The welfare optimum \(x=(1,0,0)\) and common prices \(p_n=4\)
satisfy the individual first-order conditions.
The own negative-utility Hessian is
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
1&-1/2\\
-1/2&1
\end{pmatrix}\succ0,
\]
so these conditions establish a Nash equilibrium.
Nevertheless,
\[
t_1=\frac{14}{3},\qquad
V_1(1)=\frac92,\qquad
U_1=-\frac16.
\]
Using \([0,2]\), rather than the initially recorded \([0,1]\),
makes the active allocation locally interior and removes the
price-selection ambiguity.
\ledger{4, 10, 14, 37}

Full-message uniqueness also fails.
Let
\[
N=3,\quad c=3,\quad
\mathcal X_n=[0,1],\quad
V_n(x)=2x-\frac{x^2}{2},\quad \alpha=1.
\]
Every profile \(x_n=1,\ p_n=p\), for \(p\in[0,1]\), is an
equilibrium.
Capacity binds, price derivatives vanish, and allocation utility
derivatives equal \(1-p\ge0\) at the upper boundary.
The same positive definite own Hessian proves sufficiency.
The welfare allocation is unique and zero is strictly aggregate
feasible, but equilibrium messages are not unique.
\ledger{16, 27, 37}

Additional obstacles to inheriting Q's learning proof are recorded
in \url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md}, lines 44--48:
its reduced-payment matrices do not reproduce the explicit tax;
full-map nonexpansiveness does not imply the componentwise
inequality used in its proof; and
\(\lceil0.96^{i+1}\rceil=1\) in exact arithmetic.
The preceding counterexamples do not depend on these issues.

\section{A corrected mechanism and direct implementation}

Delete the final opponent-only term from Q's explicit payment.
Writing its scale as \(\gamma>0\), the corrected tax is
\[
\begin{aligned}
t_n^c(s)
={}&\frac{\gamma}{2(N-1)}
       \sum_{j\ne n}\|p_n-p_j\|^2\\
&-\frac{\gamma}{N-1}
       p_n^\top\left(\sum_jx_j-c\right)\\
&+\frac{\gamma N}{(N-1)^2}
       \left(\sum_{j\ne n}p_j\right)^\top
       \left(x_n-\frac cN\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
At a fixed scale, the deletion changes neither own derivatives
nor best responses or equilibria.
It changes transfers, which is precisely what the participation
counterexample requires.
\ledger{5}
See \url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md}, lines 50--71.

Assume \(N\ge2\), compact convex nonnegative local sets containing
zero, normalized valuations \(V_n(0)=0\), and existence of a
planner multiplier.
Let \(f_n=-V_n\) be \(m\)-strongly convex.
For differentiable costs, the negative-utility pseudo-gradient is
\[
\begin{aligned}
F_{x_n}
&=\nabla f_n(x_n)-\frac{\gamma}{N-1}p_n
 +\frac{\gamma N}{(N-1)^2}\sum_{j\ne n}p_j,\\
F_{p_n}
&=\gamma\left[
p_n-\frac1{N-1}\sum_{j\ne n}p_j
-\frac1{N-1}\left(\sum_jx_j-c\right)
\right].
\end{aligned}
\]
Allocation subgradients replace gradients for nonsmooth costs.

Initially prices range over the nonnegative orthant.
At equilibrium,
\(F_{p_n}\ge0\) and \(p_n^\top F_{p_n}=0\).
Summing stationarity proves feasibility.
Put
\[
r=c-\sum_nx_n\ge0,\qquad
\bar p=\frac1N\sum_np_n.
\]
Summed complementarity gives
\[
0=
\frac{\gamma N}{N-1}\sum_n\|p_n-\bar p\|^2
+\frac{\gamma}{N-1}
 \left(\sum_np_n\right)^\top r.
\]
Both terms are nonnegative.
Thus prices agree, \(p_n=p\), and \(p^\top r=0\).
Allocation stationarity reduces to
\[
0\in\partial f_n(x_n)+\gamma p+
N_{\mathcal X_n}(x_n),
\]
the planner KKT conditions with multiplier \(\lambda=\gamma p\).

Conversely, any planner primal-dual optimum gives an equilibrium
when own objectives are convex; it suffices that
\(m\ge\gamma/(N-1)^2\).
All equilibria therefore implement the uniquely optimal allocation.
Prices need not be unique.
\ledger{3, 5, 8, 14, 37}
See \url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md}, lines 73--88.

At any common price, whether or not aggregate feasibility holds,
\[
t_n^c
=\gamma p^\top
\left(x_n-\frac1{N-1}\sum_{j\ne n}x_j\right).
\]
Consequently \(\sum_nt_n^c=0\).
The feasible deviation \((0,p)\) pays
\[
t_n^c((0,p),s_{-n})
=-\frac{\gamma}{N-1}p^\top\sum_{j\ne n}x_j\le0.
\]
Equilibrium optimality proves individual rationality.
This direct argument resolves the economic objection without
repairing Q's incompatible strict LMIs.
\ledger{5, 14, 27, 37}
See \url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md}, lines 90--100.

\section{What P transfers: a surrogate and economic errors}

For tail mass \(\rho_n\in(0,1)\), define
\[
C_n(x)=\operatorname{CVaR}_{\rho_n}[\ell_n(x,\xi)],
\qquad
V_n(x)=C_n(0)-C_n(x).
\]
The planner minimizes the sum of local CVaRs, rather than CVaR of
the aggregate random loss.
Deterministic payments pass through CVaR by translation equivariance.

The retained oracle results assume sample losses are
\(m\)-strongly convex, uniformly \(L_n\)-Lipschitz, and bounded by
\(U_n\) in absolute value.
The earlier versions assume these properties on a convex
neighborhood containing all queries.
This strengthens Q's expectation-only curvature assumption.
The appended feasible-query construction removes the external
neighborhood requirement.

For fixed batches and radii, P supplies the useful deterministic
surrogate
\[
\widetilde C_n(x)
=\mathbb E_{\nu,\xi^{1:s_n}}
 \widehat C_{n,s_n}(x+\delta_n\nu),
\qquad
\nu\sim\operatorname{Unif}(\mathbb B).
\]
Fresh batches and sphere directions give an unbiased one-point
gradient oracle for this surrogate.
Translation equivariance extracts a common deterministic
quadratic, so samplewise curvature survives empirical CVaR,
expectation, and smoothing.
\ledger{6, 8}

Before dividing by the radius, P's DKW argument gives
\[
\sup_x|\widetilde C_n(x)-C_n(x)|\le e_n,
\qquad
e_n=L_n\delta_n+
 \frac{U_n\sqrt{2\pi}}{\rho_n\sqrt{s_n}}.
\]
This is a uniform bound on an expected empirical objective;
it does not exchange a samplewise supremum and expectation.

Let \(\widetilde s\) be a corrected-mechanism equilibrium for
normalized surrogate valuations, and let \(x^\star\) be the true
planner optimum.
Writing
\[
\Delta_C(x)
=\sum_nC_n(x_n)-\sum_nC_n(x_n^\star),
\]
the original sandwich argument established
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\sum_n\widetilde x_n\le c,\qquad
 \sum_nt_n^c(\widetilde s)=0,\\
&0\le\Delta_C(\widetilde x)\le2\sum_ne_n,\\
&\operatorname{Regret}_n(\widetilde s)\le2e_n,\qquad
 U_n(\widetilde s)\ge-2e_n,\\
&\|\widetilde x-x^\star\|^2
 \le\frac{4\sum_ne_n}{m}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Regret permits all unilateral allocation and nonnegative-price
deviations.
The welfare proof inserts the surrogate at both allocations;
normalization constants cancel in regret; participation compares
zero and the equilibrium allocation.
Strong convexity supplies the allocation bound.
These estimates remain valid and are sharpened below.
\ledger{6, 8, 14, 17, 30}

\begin{objection}
Exact surrogate participation does not imply exact participation
under true CVaR preferences.
\end{objection}

Take \(N=3\), \(c=\gamma=m=1\), and
\(\mathcal X_n=[0,2]\), with
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_1(x,\xi)&=\frac{x^2}{2}-2x+\xi x,
&\mathbb P(\xi=\pm1)&=\frac12,\\
\ell_2(x)&=\ell_3(x)=x+\frac{x^2}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
At tail mass \(1/2\), batch size one makes expected empirical CVaR
equal expected loss.
For suitable symmetric smoothing, the unique surrogate equilibrium
is \(x=(1,0,0)\), \(p_n=1\).
Its corrected taxes are \((1,-1/2,-1/2)\).
Agent 1's true utility is \(-1/2\), while the deviation
\((x_1,p_1)=(0,1/2)\) gives utility \(1/8\).
The true regret is at least \(5/8\); price selection cannot avoid
this participation failure.
\ledger{8, 10, 14, 37}

\section{The retained learning repair}

Stack allocations before prices and set
\[
H=\operatorname{diag}
 \left(I_{NK},\frac{N-1}{N}I_{NK}\right),
\qquad G=HF.
\]
Positive scalar weights on Cartesian blocks preserve VI solutions.
In consensus directions the symmetric operator has lower bound
\(\operatorname{diag}(m,0)\).
In disagreement directions its lower bound is
\[
\begin{pmatrix}
m&-\gamma\kappa_N\\
-\gamma\kappa_N&\gamma
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\kappa_N=\frac{2N-1}{2(N-1)^2}.
\]
Thus, if
\[
0<\gamma<
\frac{4m(N-1)^4}{(2N-1)^2},
\]
there is \(q>0\) such that
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle G(s)-G(s'),s-s'\rangle
\ge q\bigl(
 \|x-x'\|^2+\|\Pi_\perp(p-p')\|^2
\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]
Here \(\Pi_\perp\) projects onto price disagreement.
This certificate also implies own-objective strong convexity.
For \(N\ge3\), \(\gamma=m\) works; \(N=2\) requires a smaller scale.
The common-price direction remains flat.
\ledger{7, 8, 14, 37}
See \url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md}, lines 102--132.

Use a common compact price box containing an uncapped equilibrium.
Comparing the capped and uncapped VI inequalities forces every
capped solution to have the same allocation and price disagreement.
Prices are common; slack resources have zero prices.
There are no additional capped equilibria under this certificate.

For strictly positive capacities and public loss bounds, a
sufficient coordinate cap is
\[
P_{\max}^{(k)}>
\frac{2\sum_nU_n}{\gamma c^{(k)}}.
\]
Testing zero in the planner dual problem bounds
\(\lambda^\top c\) by \(2\sum_nU_n\).
Public bounds are additional information assumptions.
Zero-capacity resources require separate treatment.
\ledger{8, 12, 14}
See \url{ada/mechanism_surrogate.md}, lines 134 onward.

The retained stochastic Tikhonov iteration is
\[
\begin{aligned}
s_{k+1}
&=\Pi_{\mathcal S}\left[
s_k-\eta_k\{\widehat G_k(s_k)+\varepsilon_ks_k\}
\right],\\
\eta_k&=A(k+1)^{-a},\qquad
\varepsilon_k=B(k+1)^{-b},\\
&A,B>0,\qquad \frac12<a<1,\qquad 0<b<1-a.
\end{aligned}
\]
It uses P's oracle in allocation blocks and exact payment
derivatives elsewhere.
Aggregate allocation and price information suffice.
Regularization changes learning, not the limiting tax.

Let \(z_\varepsilon\) solve the regularized VI.
The path estimate
\[
\|z_\varepsilon-z_{\varepsilon'}\|
\le
R\frac{|\varepsilon-\varepsilon'|}
        {\min(\varepsilon,\varepsilon')}
\]
implies consecutive target displacement \(O(k^{-1})\).
For \(D_k=\|s_k-z_{\varepsilon_k}\|^2\), projection and conditional
unbiasedness give, eventually,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[D_{k+1}\mid\mathcal F_k]
\le{}&(1-c_0\eta_k\varepsilon_k)D_k\\
&+O(\eta_k^2)
+O\left(\frac{k^{-2}}{\eta_k\varepsilon_k}\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
The errors are summable and
\(\sum_k\eta_k\varepsilon_k=\infty\).
An almost-supermartingale argument gives tracking.
The deterministic path selects the minimum-norm surrogate
equilibrium; compactness upgrades almost-sure convergence to
mean-square convergence.
\ledger{7, 8, 14, 37}

This applies established stochastic regularization.
With fixed biased batches, the limit is a surrogate equilibrium,
not necessarily a true equilibrium.

\section{Retained extension: one-sided bias and every finite batch}

Put
\[
f_{n,s}=\mathbb E\widehat C_{n,s},\qquad
a_n=L_n\delta_n,\qquad
b_n=\frac{U_n\sqrt{2\pi}}{\rho_n\sqrt{s_n}}.
\]
Expectation of an infimum is no greater than the infimum of its
expectation, so the CVaR representation gives \(f_{n,s}\le C_n\).
Together with DKW and centered convex smoothing,
\[
\begin{aligned}
C_n-b_n
&\le f_{n,s_n}\le\widetilde C_n\\
&\le\mathbb E_\nu C_n(\,\cdot+\delta_n\nu)
\le C_n+a_n.
\end{aligned}
\]
The discrepancy lies in \([-b_n,a_n]\), so its oscillation is at
most \(e_n=a_n+b_n\).
This removes the factor two from the preceding sandwich:
\[
\begin{aligned}
0&\le\Delta_C(\widetilde x)\le\sum_ne_n,\\
\operatorname{Regret}_n(\widetilde s)&\le e_n,\qquad
U_n(\widetilde s)\ge-e_n,\\
\|\widetilde x-x^\star\|^2&\le\frac{2\sum_ne_n}{m}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Feasibility and budget balance remain exact.
There is no inverse radius in these economic bounds, although the
oracle variance depends on that radius.
\ledger{17, 27, 37}

For symmetric Bernoulli noise and tail mass \(1/2\), let
\(B_s\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(s,1/2)\).
Then
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat{\operatorname{CVaR}}_{1/2}(\xi)
&=1-\frac4s(s/2-B_s)_+,\\
\mathbb E\widehat{\operatorname{CVaR}}_{1/2}(\xi)
&=1-d_s,\\
d_s&=\frac2s\mathbb E|B_s-s/2|>0.
\end{aligned}
\]
Fractional weighting covers odd sample sizes.
For even sizes,
\[
d_{2r}=\binom{2r}{r}4^{-r}
\sim\frac1{\sqrt{\pi r}},
\]
and \(d_{2r+1}=d_{2r}\) for \(r\ge1\), with \(d_1=1\).

For each finite \(s\), retain
\(N=3\), \(c=\gamma=m=1\), \(\mathcal X_n=[0,2]\), and choose
\[
\begin{aligned}
\ell_1(x,\xi)
&=\frac{x^2}{2}+\left(-1+\frac{d_s}{2}\right)x+\xi x,\\
\ell_2(x)&=\ell_3(x)=x+\frac{x^2}{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]
On nonnegative allocations,
\[
C_1(x)=\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac{d_s}{2}x,\qquad
f_{1,s}(x)=\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{d_s}{2}x.
\]
For \(0<\delta_1<d_s/2\), the surrogate optimum remains
\[
\widetilde x=(d_s/2,0,0),\qquad p_n=0.
\]
Capacity is slack and taxes vanish.
The true optimum is zero, and
\[
-U_1(\widetilde s)
=\operatorname{Regret}_1(\widetilde s)
=\Delta_C(\widetilde x)
=\frac{3d_s^2}{8}.
\]
The zero-allocation, zero-price deviation attains regret; the notes
also check deviations beyond capacity after optimizing own price.
\ledger{23, 27, 37}

The family has uniform loss, Lipschitz, and curvature bounds.
Its linear coefficient varies with \(s\).
It disproves uniform exact participation for this estimator at
every finite batch size, rather than asserting failure on one
fixed instance for all batches or impossibility for all estimators.
Its economic distortion is \(\Theta(s^{-1})\), whereas value bias
is \(\Theta(s^{-1/2})\).

\section{Retained extension: multilevel estimation and finite cost}

For terminal batch \(2^J\), define coupled-half increments
\[
\Delta_j=\widehat C_{2^j}
-\frac12\left(
 \widehat C_{2^{j-1}}^{(1)}
+\widehat C_{2^{j-1}}^{(2)}
\right).
\]
Draw \(L\in\{1,\ldots,J\}\) with
\[
q_j=\frac{2^{-j}}{1-2^{-J}},
\qquad
Y_J=\widehat C_1^{(0)}+\frac{\Delta_L}{q_L},
\]
using an independent baseline.
Telescoping and squared DKW estimates give
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb EY_J&=\mathbb E\widehat C_{2^J},\\
\mathbb E[\text{loss evaluations}]
&=1+\frac{J}{1-2^{-J}},\\
\mathbb E\Delta_j^2&=O(2^{-j}),\qquad
\mathbb EY_J^2=O(J).
\end{aligned}
\]
The one-point gradient second moment is \(O(J/\delta^2)\).
The expected surrogate is unchanged, even though a realized
estimator need not be convex.
Worst-case work remains \(2^J+1\).
\ledger{18, 27, 37}

For the Bernoulli example,
\[
\mathbb E\Delta_j=\Theta(2^{-j/2}),\qquad
\mathbb E\Delta_j^2=\Theta(2^{-j}).
\]
For any single-random-level probabilities,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\left(\sum_{j=1}^M
 \sqrt{2^j\mathbb E\Delta_j^2}\right)^2
\le
\left(\sum_{j=1}^M q_j2^j\right)
\left(\sum_{j=1}^M
 \frac{\mathbb E\Delta_j^2}{q_j}\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
The left side is \(\Theta(M^2)\).
Infinite telescoping in this family cannot have both finite
expected work and finite variance.
At terminal level \(J\), the product is
\(\Omega(J^2)\), attained in order by the displayed construction.
This is an estimator-class lower bound.
\ledger{23, 27, 37}

For finite-time learning, hold \(h>0\) fixed.
The solution \(z_h=(x_h,p_h)\) of
\(\operatorname{VI}(G+hI,\mathcal S)\) has common prices
\[
p_h=\frac{\gamma}{Nh}
 \left[\sum_nx_{h,n}-c\right]_+
\]
and minimizes, in allocation,
\[
\sum_n\widetilde C_n(x_n)
+\frac h2\|x\|^2
+\frac{\gamma^2}{2Nh}
 \left\|\left[\sum_nx_n-c\right]_+\right\|^2.
\]
Testing stationarity against zero gives
\[
\gamma p_h^\top c+Nh\|p_h\|^2+h\|x_h\|^2
\le2\sum_nU_n.
\]
The price cap is uniform in \(h\); capacity violation is \(O(h)\);
budget balance is exact at \(z_h\).
\ledger{19}

With \(w=(N-1)/N\), regularization subtracts
\[
\frac h2\|x_n\|^2+\frac h{2w}\|p_n\|^2
\]
from surrogate utility.
Comparison with \((0,p_h)\) proves exact surrogate participation
there, true utility at least \(-e_n\), and true regret at most
\(e_n+O(h)\).

The correction in \ledger{21} matters:
unregularized improvement is bounded by the deviation penalty
minus the current penalty.
Deviation prices are bounded because the price best response is
\[
p_n'=\left[
\frac{\sum_{j\ne n}p_j}{N-1}
+\frac{x_n'+\sum_{j\ne n}x_j-c}{N-1}
\right]_+.
\]
Compact allocations and capped opponents bound this expression.
See \url{ada/finite_cost_extension.md}, lines 195--229.

For
\[
\begin{aligned}
s_{t+1}
&=\Pi_{\mathcal S}
 [s_t-\eta_t\{\widehat G_t(s_t)+hs_t\}],\\
\eta_t&=\frac1{h(t+2)},
\end{aligned}
\]
a conditional update second-moment bound \(B_h^2\) gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
r_{t+1}
&\le\left(1-\frac2{t+2}\right)r_t
+\frac{B_h^2}{h^2(t+2)^2},\\
r_t&=\mathbb E\|s_t-z_h\|^2,\\
r_T&\le\frac{\max\{r_0,B_h^2/h^2\}}{T+1}.
\end{aligned}
\]
No operator Lipschitz constant is required.
Economic continuity on the capped state and bounded deviation
sets yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E\operatorname{Regret}_n(s_T)
&\le e_n+O(h+\sqrt{r_T}),\\
\mathbb E[-U_n(s_T)]_+
&\le e_n+O(\sqrt{r_T}),\\
\mathbb E\left|\sum_nt_n^c(s_T)\right|
&=O(\sqrt{r_T}),\\
\mathbb E\left\|
 [\textstyle\sum_nx_{T,n}-c]_+\right\|
&=O(h+\sqrt{r_T}),\\
\mathbb E[\Delta_C(x_T)]_+
&\le\sum_ne_n+O(h+\sqrt{r_T}),\\
\mathbb E\|\Pi_\perp p_T\|^2&\le r_T,\\
\mathbb E\|x_T-x^\star\|^2
&=O\left(r_T+h+\sum_ne_n\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
Positive welfare excess is used because finite-time allocations
can violate capacity.
\ledger{19, 21, 27}
See \url{ada/finite_cost_extension.md}, lines 205--252.

Originally,
\(h\asymp\delta\asymp\epsilon\) and
\(2^J\asymp\epsilon^{-2}\) gave multilevel total expected work
\[
O\left(\epsilon^{-6}\log^2(1/\epsilon)\right).
\]
Plain batches in the same regularized one-point scheme gave
\(O(\epsilon^{-8})\).
Geometric growing-batch stages have the same final-order upper
bound because the last stage dominates.
These conservative bounds remain valid for those choices.
Sharp bias later improves the plain-batch bound to
\(O(\epsilon^{-7})\); different averaged learners improve further.
\ledger{19, 25, 33}

\section{Retained extension: private thresholds remove batch bias}

\begin{objection}
The empirical-CVaR counterexamples do not imply that constant-sample
learning must retain empirical-risk bias.
\end{objection}

Extract the deterministic quadratic:
\[
g_n(x,\xi)=\ell_n(x,\xi)-\frac m2\|x\|^2.
\]
Assume \(|g_n|\le B_n\), with Lipschitz constant \(L_{g,n}\).
A private threshold in \([-B_n,B_n]\) gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
C_n(x)=\frac m2\|x\|^2+
\min_{\tau\in[-B_n,B_n]}
\left\{\tau+\frac1{\rho_n}
 \mathbb E[g_n(x,\xi)-\tau]_+\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Extracting curvature is essential: a threshold hinge alone need
not be strongly convex in allocation.

Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
Q_{n,\delta}(x,\tau)
={}&\frac m2\|x\|^2+\tau\\
&+\frac1{\rho_n}
 \mathbb E_{\nu,\xi}[g_n(x+\delta_n\nu,\xi)-\tau]_+,
\end{aligned}
\]
with ball-uniform \(\nu\), and
\[
C_{n,\delta}^{\mathrm{lift}}(x)
=\min_{\tau\in[-B_n,B_n]}Q_{n,\delta}(x,\tau).
\]
Subtracting \(m\|x\|^2/2\) leaves a jointly convex function.
Threshold differentiability or curvature is unnecessary.
Jensen, monotonicity, and translation equivariance imply
\[
0\le C_{n,\delta}^{\mathrm{lift}}(x)-C_n(x)
\le A_n,\qquad A_n=L_{g,n}\delta_n.
\]
There is no inverse tail-mass factor in this approximation bound;
oracle constants still depend on tail mass.
\ledger{20, 22, 27, 37}
See \url{ada/private_threshold_oracle.md}, lines 15--44.

With independent fresh scenarios, sphere direction \(u\), and ball
perturbation \(\nu\), use
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat q_x
&=mx+\frac K{\rho_n\delta_n}
 [g_n(x+\delta_nu,\xi_1)-\tau]_+u,\\
\widehat q_\tau
&=1-\frac1{\rho_n}
 \mathbf1\{g_n(x+\delta_n\nu,\xi_2)>\tau\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The expectations form a joint subgradient of \(Q_{n,\delta}\).
At atoms, strict inequality chooses zero as the hinge subgradient
at equality.
Allocation differentiability after smoothing and bounded
subgradient integration justify compatibility.

The sphere and ball laws are deliberately different.
Using the sphere query for the threshold update would generally
target another objective.
There are exactly two loss evaluations per agent per update, and
\[
\|\widehat q_x\|
\le mR_{x,n}+\frac{2KB_n}{\rho_n\delta_n},
\qquad
|\widehat q_\tau|\le1+\rho_n^{-1}.
\]
Thus the second moment is \(O(\delta_{\min}^{-2})\), without batch
bias, scenario gradients, or scenario replay.
\ledger{22, 27, 37}
See \url{ada/private_threshold_oracle.md}, lines 46--64.

The joint subgradients satisfy
\[
\begin{aligned}
\langle q_n-q_n',
 (x_n-x_n',\tau_n-\tau_n')\rangle
\ge m\|x_n-x_n'\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
Weighting only prices by \((N-1)/N\) therefore preserves the
earlier control of allocations and price disagreement.
Threshold and common-price directions can remain flat.
Thresholds do not enter taxes and remain private.

Without regularization, reduction over thresholds gives the
corrected game with preferences \(C_{n,\delta}^{\mathrm{lift}}\).
Its equilibria have exact feasibility and budget balance, true
regret at most \(A_n\), true utility at least \(-A_n\), and welfare
loss at most \(\sum_nA_n\).
\ledger{22, 27}

For the extended regularized VI, prices remain
\[
p_h=\frac{\gamma}{Nh}
 [\textstyle\sum_nx_{h,n}-c]_+,
\]
and \((x_h,\tau_h)\) minimizes
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\sum_nQ_{n,\delta}(x_n,\tau_n)
+\frac h2(\|x\|^2+\|\tau\|^2)\\
&\qquad+
\frac{\gamma^2}{2Nh}
 \left\|[\textstyle\sum_nx_n-c]_+\right\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
If \(\overline Q_n\) uniformly bounds \(|Q_{n,\delta}|\), then
\[
\gamma p_h^\top c+Nh\|p_h\|^2
\le2\sum_n\overline Q_n+\frac h2\sum_nB_n^2.
\]
For \(0<h\le1\), a coordinate cap exceeding
\[
\frac{2\sum_n\overline Q_n+\tfrac12\sum_nB_n^2}
     {\gamma c^{(k)}}
\]
suffices uniformly.
At \(z_h\), capacity violation is \(O(h)\), budget balance exact,
true regret \(A_n+O(h)\), and
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta_C(x_h)
&\le\sum_nA_n+
 \frac h2\left(R_x^2+\sum_nB_n^2\right),\\
U_n(z_h)&\ge-A_n-\frac h2B_n^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
A threshold change at zero can incur a regularization penalty,
so exact surrogate participation at the regularized lifted point
is not asserted.

The same projection recursion gives
\[
r_T=\mathbb E\|z_T-z_h\|^2
\le\frac{\max\{r_0,B_h^2/h^2\}}{T+1},
\qquad B_h^2=O(\delta_{\min}^{-2}).
\]
Economic errors gain \(O(\sqrt{r_T})\), while
\[
\mathbb E\|x_T-x^\star\|^2
=O\left(r_T+h+\sum_nA_n\right),
\qquad
\mathbb E\|\Pi_\perp p_T\|^2\le r_T.
\]
Choosing \(h\asymp\delta\asymp\epsilon\) gives the retained
\(O(\epsilon^{-6})\) constant-sample total-query guarantee.
\ledger{22, 25, 27}

Geometric accuracy stages recover true optimal allocations at
stage ends almost surely and in mean square.
Summable tracking estimates, Markov's inequality, and
Borel--Cantelli control stochastic errors.
Vanishing regret makes message cluster points true equilibria.
This does not prove convergence of all prices when prices are
nonunique, or of every within-stage iterate.
\ledger{22, 25}

\section{Verification retained from the preceding note}

The original exact checks cover payment identities for
\(N=2,\ldots,8\), metric calculations for \(N=2,\ldots,10\),
and the initial participation counterexamples.
See \url{ada/algebra_check_01.log}.
Review \ledger{14} independently checked the mechanism,
weighted monotonicity, capped VI, and tracking proof.

A ten-seed experiment used 200,000 iterations per seed, batch size
one, and radius \(0.5\).
Mean terminal true utility was approximately \(-0.4954\), against
the exact limit \(-0.5\); mean terminal state distance was
approximately \(0.0507\).
Finite-time capacity violations remained.
Equal initial prices are preserved by that iteration, so tiny
budget errors are not independent convergence evidence.
\ledger{11}
See \url{ada/toy_learning_01.log}.

Further checks cover binomial identities through size 64,
multilevel moments through level 7, telescoping and work formulas,
the scalar stochastic-approximation induction, and regularized KKT
calculations.
See \url{ada/finite_cost_checks_01.log}.
The threshold checks include 275625 supporting-plane and curvature
comparisons and positive-mass threshold atoms:
\url{ada/threshold_oracle_checks_01.log}.
They support selected calculations, rather than replacing proofs.
\ledger{19, 25}

Review \ledger{27} reports rechecking the threshold oracle at atoms,
uniform cap, uncapped-game regret comparison, finite-time recursion,
and finite-batch family without finding an error.
Review \ledger{37} similarly reports rechecking the next extension,
retained in the following sections.

