\section{Retained extension: Lipschitz bias without atom assumptions}

Review \ledger{27} proposed comparing empirical and population
tail weights at gradient level.
Direct matching raised concerns about ties and batch-dependent
selections.
The notes instead use deterministic spectral weights.
\ledger{28, 29}

Fix \(\rho,s\), and a pathwise \(L\)-Lipschitz loss.
Let \(q_x(u)\) be its generalized quantile.
Common-scenario coupling gives
\[
|\ell(x,\xi)-\ell(y,\xi)|\le L\|x-y\|,
\]
hence, by monotonicity and translation equivariance of quantiles,
\[
|q_x(u)-q_y(u)|\le L\|x-y\|,\qquad 0<u<1.
\]
No density, unique quantile, or differentiable loss is needed.

Write \(k=\rho s\), \(j=\lfloor k\rfloor\), \(a=k-j\), and let
\(b_{r,s}\) be the beta density of the \(r\)-th smallest of
\(s\) independent uniforms.
Then
\[
C(x)=\int_0^1q_x(u)w(u)\,du,\qquad
f_s(x)=\int_0^1q_x(u)w_s(u)\,du,
\]
where
\[
\begin{aligned}
w(u)&=\rho^{-1}\mathbf1\{u>1-\rho\},\\
w_s(u)&=\frac1k\left[
 \sum_{r=s-j+1}^s b_{r,s}(u)+a b_{s-j,s}(u)
\right].
\end{aligned}
\]
An empty sum is zero.
Fractional weighting covers noninteger tail counts;
generalized quantiles cover atoms.
The spectral representation itself is already in Levy et al.,
\url{../../sources/2010.05893.tex}, lines 4770--4830.
\ledger{29}

Because the beta densities sum to \(s\),
\[
0\le w_s\le\rho^{-1},\qquad \int_0^1w_s=1.
\]
The difference \(w-w_s\) changes sign only at \(1-\rho\).
For \(B\sim\operatorname{Binomial}(s,\rho)\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
M_{s,\rho}:=\|w-w_s\|_1
&=2\int_0^{1-\rho}w_s(u)\,du\\
&=\frac{2\mathbb E[(\rho s-B)_+]}{\rho s}\\
&\le\min\left\{2,
 \sqrt{\frac{1-\rho}{\rho s}}\right\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Apply the weights to a Bernoulli loss of success probability
\(\rho\): its empirical CVaR is
\(\min\{B/(\rho s),1\}\).
Centering of \(B-\rho s\) and its variance prove the bound.

Thus \(D_s=C-f_s\) is Lipschitz:
\[
|D_s(x)-D_s(y)|
\le \Gamma_s\|x-y\|,
\qquad \Gamma_s=LM_{s,\rho}.
\]
Any common deterministic loss term cancels from \(D_s\).
After extracting the quadratic, a residual-loss Lipschitz
constant can therefore replace \(L\).

On an open neighborhood where the convex objectives are finite,
directional derivatives are support functions of their
subdifferentials.
The same inequality gives
\[
|C'(x;v)-f_s'(x;v)|\le\Gamma_s\|v\|,
\]
and Hausdorff distance at most \(\Gamma_s\) between
\(\partial C(x)\) and \(\partial f_s(x)\).
This concerns the deterministic expected-batch objective, not
every realized empirical batch.
The economic proof needs only bias Lipschitzness.
\ledger{29, 34, 37}
See \url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, lines 7--54.

\section{Retained extension: sharp economic distortion}

Let
\[
a_n=L_n\delta_n,\qquad
\Gamma_n=L_nM_{s_n,\rho_n}.
\]
Centered convex smoothing gives
\(0\le\widetilde C_n-f_{n,s_n}\le a_n\), hence
\[
\begin{aligned}
&[C_n(x)-\widetilde C_n(x)]
-[C_n(y)-\widetilde C_n(y)]\\
&\qquad\le a_n+\Gamma_n\|x-y\|.
\end{aligned}
\]

At an exact surrogate equilibrium, a unilateral increment
\((d,q)\) in allocation and price has surrogate improvement at most
\[
-\frac12\left[
m\|d\|^2-\frac{2\gamma}{N-1}\langle d,q\rangle
+\gamma\|q\|^2
\right].
\]
Eliminating \(q\) leaves curvature
\[
\kappa=m-\frac{\gamma}{(N-1)^2}>0.
\]
The weighted monotonicity certificate ensures this positivity.
Completing the square against the Lipschitz bias gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Regret}_n(\widetilde s)
&\le a_n+\frac{\Gamma_n^2}{2\kappa},\\
U_n(\widetilde s)
&\ge-a_n-\frac{\Gamma_n^2}{2\kappa}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Surrogate planner optimality similarly contributes a negative
allocation quadratic, giving
\[
0\le\Delta_C(\widetilde x)
\le\sum_n\left(a_n+\frac{\Gamma_n^2}{2m}\right).
\]
Feasibility and budget balance remain exact.
Economic distortion has order
\[
O\left(\sum_n\delta_n+\sum_n s_n^{-1}\right)
\]
for fixed curvature and tail masses.
The older sandwich remains useful as curvature vanishes.
\ledger{30, 37}
See \url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, lines 58--105.

Both parameter orders occur in the stated surrogate class.
In the finite-batch family, choose
\(\delta_s=d_s^3/4\).
The exact surrogate minimum is unchanged,
\(\delta_s=o(s^{-1})\), and the economic errors
\(3d_s^2/8=\Theta(s^{-1})\) isolate sampling distortion.

For smoothing, let
\[
C_1(x)=\frac{x^2}{2}+|x|-\frac x2,\qquad
C_2(x)=C_3(x)=x+\frac{x^2}{2},
\]
with \(N=3\), \(m=\gamma=c=1\), and local intervals \([0,2]\).
The true optimum is zero.
Uniform-ball smoothing gives, for \(0\le x<\delta\),
\[
\widetilde C_1'(x)=x+\frac x\delta-\frac12.
\]
For small \(\delta\), the surrogate equilibrium has
\[
\widetilde x_1=\frac{\delta}{2(1+\delta)},
\qquad \widetilde x_2=\widetilde x_3=0,\qquad p_n=0.
\]
Regret, participation shortfall, and welfare loss all equal
\[
\frac{\delta}{4(1+\delta)}
+\frac{\delta^2}{8(1+\delta)^2}
=\Theta(\delta).
\]
Joint price deviations do not improve on the true zero response.
This example uses negative oracle queries around a boundary kink;
it is not a feasible-query lower bound.
The appended round supplies a different contraction example.
\ledger{30, 37}
See \url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, lines 107--127.

\section{Retained extension: a saddle gap controls mechanism regret}

Use the lifted planner with \(y=(x,\tau)\in Y\):
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_\delta(y,\lambda)
&=\sum_nQ_{n,\delta}(x_n,\tau_n)
+\lambda^\top r(x),\\
r(x)&=\sum_nx_n-c,\qquad
\lambda\in[0,\Lambda]^K.
\end{aligned}
\]
Here \(r\) denotes excess demand.
Choose the cap with fixed margin \(b>0\) above a planner multiplier,
uniformly over the radii.

Agents perform projected stochastic descent; the manager updates
the common multiplier from aggregate demand:
\[
\begin{aligned}
y_{t+1}
&=\Pi_Y[y_t-\eta\widehat\nabla_y
                  \mathcal L_\delta(y_t,\lambda_t)],\\
\lambda_{t+1}
&=\Pi_{[0,\Lambda]^K}[\lambda_t+\eta r(x_t)].
\end{aligned}
\]
The primal notation includes a joint subgradient at threshold
atoms.
Prices are reported as \(p_n^t=\lambda_t/\gamma\);
thresholds remain private.

For averaged iterates, define the restricted saddle gap
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal G_T
={}&\max_{\lambda\in[0,\Lambda]^K}
 \mathcal L_\delta(\bar y_T,\lambda)\\
&-\min_{y\in Y}\mathcal L_\delta(y,\bar\lambda_T).
\end{aligned}
\]
This is a prescribed learning protocol, not proof that following
each transient update is strategically optimal.
\ledger{31, 32, 37}

The gap splits as
\(\mathcal G_T=\mathcal A+\mathcal D\), where
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A&=\sum_n\mathcal A_n,\\
\mathcal A_n
&=Q_{n,\delta}(\bar x_n,\bar\tau_n)
+\bar\lambda^\top\bar x_n\\
&\quad-\min_{x_n,\tau_n}
 \{Q_{n,\delta}(x_n,\tau_n)+\bar\lambda^\top x_n\},\\
\mathcal D
&=\Lambda\|r(\bar x)_+\|_1
-\bar\lambda^\top r(\bar x).
\end{aligned}
\]
All terms are nonnegative, and
\(\mathcal D\ge\bar\lambda^\top r_-\).

Let \(\widehat C_n=\min_\tau Q_{n,\delta}\).
Strong convexity around its local priced minimizer gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\widehat C_n(\bar x_n)+\bar\lambda^\top\bar x_n
-\widehat C_n(x_n')-\bar\lambda^\top x_n'\\
&\qquad\le
2\mathcal A_n-\frac m4\|x_n'-\bar x_n\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
At common price \(p=\bar\lambda/\gamma\), set
\(d=x_n'-\bar x_n\) and \(p_n'=p+q\ge0\).
Changing own price from \(p\) to \(p+q\), at the deviating
allocation, changes the tax by
\[
\frac\gamma2\|q\|^2
-\frac\gamma{N-1}q^\top(r+d).
\]
If \(\gamma\le m(N-1)^2/4\), then
\[
\frac\gamma{N-1}q^\top d
\le\frac m4\|d\|^2+
 \frac{\gamma^2}{m(N-1)^2}\|q\|^2.
\]
Also,
\[
q^\top r\le\|q\|\|r_+\|+p^\top r_-.
\]
Optimizing the remaining quadratic yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Regret}_n(\bar s_T)
\le{}&2\mathcal A_n+a_n+\frac{\mathcal D}{N-1}\\
&+\frac\gamma{(N-1)^2}\|r_+\|^2,
\qquad a_n=L_{g,n}\delta_n.
\end{aligned}
\]
The learning multiplier is capped; unilateral prices in this
bound are unrestricted above.
\ledger{32, 37}
See \url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, lines 129--203.

Weak duality and the cap margin give
\[
\mathcal G_T\ge b\|r_+\|_1.
\]
Compact allocations imply
\(\|r_+\|^2\le R_r\|r_+\|_1\).
Thus true regret is \(O(\mathcal G_T+a_n)\).
The zero-allocation deviation bounds participation shortfall by
regret.
Common prices give exact budget balance.
Moreover,
\[
\Delta_C(\bar x_T)\le\mathcal G_T+\sum_na_n,
\qquad
\|\bar x_T-x^\star\|^2
=O\left(\mathcal G_T+\sum_na_n\right).
\]
The allocation bound combines the welfare and capacity estimates
with true planner KKT curvature.
Welfare is interpreted through its positive part at possibly
infeasible outputs.
\ledger{32, 37}
See \url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, lines 205--225.

\section{Retained extension: expected gap and the earlier comparisons}

For diameter \(D_Z\) of the product domain and conditional oracle
second moment \(B^2\), ordinary projected averaging gives
\[
\mathbb E\mathcal G_T
\le
\frac{D_Z^2}{2\eta T}
+\frac{\eta B^2}{2}
+\frac{D_ZB}{\sqrt T}.
\]
The last term controls the comparator selected after seeing the
trajectory.
Writing the oracle as conditional mean plus martingale difference
\(\zeta_t\), its predictable inner-product part has expectation
zero, while the remaining supremum is bounded by
\(D_Z\|\sum_t\zeta_t\|\).
Martingale orthogonality supplies the displayed bound.
Expectation and maximum are not interchanged.
\ledger{32, 37}

For the threshold oracle, \(B^2=O(\delta_{\min}^{-2})\).
Taking
\[
\eta=\frac{D_Z}{B\sqrt T},\qquad
\delta\asymp\epsilon,\qquad
T=O(\epsilon^{-4})
\]
gives expected \(O(\epsilon)\) regret, participation shortfall,
capacity violation, positive welfare excess, and squared allocation
error.
Budget balance is exact.
With two queries per agent per update, total query cost is
\(O(\epsilon^{-4})\).
This remains a valid averaged guarantee, distinct from the retained
regularized last-iterate bound.
\ledger{32, 35}

For empirical CVaR, reserve allocation curvature for both price
deviations and the Lipschitz bias:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Gamma_n\|d\|
&\le\frac m8\|d\|^2+\frac{2\Gamma_n^2}{m},\\
\frac\gamma{N-1}q^\top d
&\le\frac m8\|d\|^2+
 \frac{2\gamma^2}{m(N-1)^2}\|q\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
Under \(\gamma\le m(N-1)^2/8\), along with the earlier certificate,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Regret}_n
\le{}&2\mathcal A_n+a_n+\frac{2\Gamma_n^2}{m}
+\frac{\mathcal D}{N-1}\\
&+\frac\gamma{(N-1)^2}\|r_+\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
Welfare follows by the corresponding curvature comparison with
the true feasible optimum.
The economic error is
\(O(\mathcal G_T+\sum_n\delta_n+\sum_n\Gamma_n^2)\).
\ledger{33, 37}

For the \emph{unbaselined one-point} batch oracle, the preceding
ordinary saddle bound gave, with
\(s\asymp\epsilon^{-1}\),
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Empirical batches}&O(\epsilon^{-5}),\\
\text{Multilevel values}
 &O(\epsilon^{-4}\log^2(1/\epsilon)),\\
\text{Private thresholds}&O(\epsilon^{-4}).
\end{array}
\]
The multilevel count is expected work.
These are valid upper bounds for the specified estimators.
Review \ledger{37} correctly objected that they do not establish
a threshold exponent advantage against symmetric differences.
That interpretation is withdrawn by \ledger{39}.
The appended comparison replaces it.

\section{Retained verification and additional source comparison}

The sharp-bias and saddle checks cover 120 spectral identities,
including fractional tails; 4335 atomic crossing-loss inequalities;
19683 unrestricted joint-regret inequalities; and 39366
Lipschitz-bias variants.
The price best response was optimized analytically rather than
over a price grid.
See \url{ada/check_sharp_bias_saddle.py} and
\url{ada/sharp_bias_saddle_checks_01.log}.
The kink formula was checked as well.
No saddle-learner convergence experiment was recorded.
\ledger{33}

Review \ledger{37} reports independent rederivation of the
fractional spectral identity, curvature conversion, joint-regret
bound, cap-margin estimate, martingale term, and tightness families.
Thus the previous note's request for that particular audit has
been answered in the ledger.

Additional full-text comparisons narrowed the novelty claim:
\begin{itemize}
\item Levy et al.,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.05893},
already give the spectral representation and value-bias
tightness; see pooled source lines 2133--2145, 2190--2199,
and 4770--4830.
The proposed application is decisionwise bias Lipschitzness
followed by economic curvature conversion.

\item Mehta et al.,
\emph{Stochastic Optimization for Spectral Risk Measures},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.05149}.
See pooled source lines 530--567, 1054--1158, and 1207--1218.
The inspected argument already uses expected minibatch surrogates
and strong convexity, with a finite-dataset, without-replacement
uniform value-bias comparison.

\item Tam Le,
\emph{Convergence of empirical subgradients for optimal
transport-based objectives},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.28134}.
See pooled source lines 390--449.
The inspected result concerns asymptotic empirical spectral-risk
subgradient graph inclusion for differentiable losses.
The present bound concerns a deterministic expected-batch
objective with an explicit rate under pathwise Lipschitzness.
\end{itemize}
These are scoped comparisons, not certification of novelty.
See \url{ada/sharp_bias_and_saddle.md}, lines 270--284.
\ledger{29, 34}

Earlier Hurwicz, Chen, and Koshal requests still failed.
The recorded sources include
\url{https://yanchen.people.si.umich.edu/published/Chen_ET_2002.pdf},
