\section{Appended round: an independent symmetric-batch baseline}

Review \ledger{37} proposed symmetric differences with independent
batches.
The new note confirms the objection and corrects the comparison.
Let \(f_s=\mathbb E\widehat C_s\), and let \(f_{s,\delta}\) be its
ball smoothing.
For a sphere-uniform \(u\), query independent batches at the two
points and use
\[
\widehat g(x)=\frac{d}{2\delta}
 \left[\widehat C_s(x+\delta u)
       -\widehat C_s'(x-\delta u)\right]u.
\]
Sphere symmetry gives
\[
\mathbb E[\widehat g(x)\mid x]
=\nabla f_{s,\delta}(x).
\]
It targets exactly the same surrogate as P's one-point oracle.
Neither common random numbers nor scenario replay is needed.
\ledger{38, 39}

Integrating the DKW tail bound yields
\[
\mathbb E|\widehat C_s(z)-C(z)|^2
\le\frac{4U^2}{\rho^2s},
\qquad
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat C_s(z))
\le\frac{4U^2}{\rho^2s}.
\]
Expected empirical CVaR is \(L\)-Lipschitz.
Conditionally on \(u\), the mean difference is at most
\(2L\delta\).
Independence of the batches therefore gives
\[
\mathbb E\|\widehat g(x)\|^2
\le d^2L^2+\frac{2d^2U^2}{\rho^2s\delta^2}.
\]
Each update costs \(2s\) loss queries.

With \(s\asymp\epsilon^{-1}\) and
\(\delta\asymp\epsilon\), this second moment is
\(O(\epsilon^{-1})\).
Even the ordinary saddle learner then needs only
\(O(\epsilon^{-3})\) updates, hence
\(O(\epsilon^{-4})\) total queries.
It matches the earlier threshold total-query exponent.
Thresholds retain constant per-update work and no fixed empirical
surrogate bias, rather than an established exponent advantage.
\ledger{39}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 5--32.

\section{Appended round: locally feasible queries}

\begin{objection}
The earlier oracle theorems query outside nonnegative allocation
sets, while simply shrinking the sets removes the zero deviation
used for participation.
\end{objection}

The new construction handles both issues.
Let
\[
E_n=\operatorname{aff}(\mathcal X_n),\qquad d_n=\dim E_n.
\]
Since zero belongs to \(\mathcal X_n\), \(E_n\) is a linear
subspace.
For \(d_n>0\), choose a relative-interior anchor \(a_n\) and
radius \(r_n>0\) such that
\[
a_n+r_n\mathbb B_{E_n}\subseteq\mathcal X_n.
\]
Assume strictly positive capacities.
Scaling an interior point toward zero permits
\[
a_n^{(k)}\le\frac{c^{(k)}}{2N},
\qquad
c-\sum_na_n\ge c/2.
\]
The scaled relative ball remains in the set by convexity.

Each agent can choose its anchor using the public capacity vector.
Anchors and affine hulls need not be disclosed to the manager.
This relaxes the unnecessarily public-anchor wording in
\ledger{40}; public bounds needed for the multiplier cap remain
separate assumptions.
\ledger{40, 44}

Use a common contraction fraction \(0<\theta<1\):
\[
\mathcal X_n^\theta
=(1-\theta)\mathcal X_n+\theta a_n,
\qquad
\Phi_n^\theta(x)=(1-\theta)x+\theta a_n.
\]
Choose \(0<\delta_n\le\theta r_n/2\).
For \(x=(1-\theta)y+\theta a_n\) and
\(v\in\mathbb B_{E_n}\),
\[
x+\delta_nv
=(1-\theta)y+
\theta\left(a_n+\frac{\delta_n}{\theta}v\right)
\in\mathcal X_n.
\]
A relative margin remains, so smoothing identities hold near
every center.
Use sphere and ball laws in \(E_n\), replace ambient dimension by
\(d_n\), and project allocation updates onto
\(\mathcal X_n^\theta\).
For \(d_n=0\), allocation is identically zero and its constant
objective and threshold can be omitted.

Only boundedness, Lipschitzness, and samplewise strong convexity
on \(\mathcal X_n\) are required.
All loss queries are locally feasible.
Separate perturbed queries need not satisfy the aggregate capacity
constraint.
\ledger{40, 44}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 34--59.

\subsection{Uniform Slater point and multiplier cap}

The fixed anchor belongs to every contracted set:
\(\Phi_n^\theta(a_n)=a_n\).
Its aggregate slack is at least \(c/2\), independently of
\(\theta\).
Let \(\widehat C_n\) denote either reduced lifted risk or the
empirical-smoothed risk, with uniform bounds
\(|\widehat C_n|\le M_n\).
Any optimal contracted-planner multiplier satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
\lambda_\theta^{\star\top}
 \left(c-\sum_na_n\right)
&\le\sum_n\widehat C_n(a_n)
       -\widehat C_\theta^\star\\
&\le2\sum_nM_n.
\end{aligned}
\]
Consequently,
\[
\lambda_\theta^{\star(k)}
\le\frac{4\sum_nM_n}{c^{(k)}}.
\]
A multiplier box exceeding these bounds has a fixed cap margin.
The capacity estimate remains uniform as \(\theta\downarrow0\).
The Slater comparator is the fixed anchor, not the contracted
zero allocation.
\ledger{40, 44}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 61--75.

\subsection{Original deviations and participation}

Against capped common price \(p\), a best-response price at any
original allocation \(x_n'\in\mathcal X_n\) is
\[
p_n'=\left[
p+\frac{\sum_{j\ne n}x_j+x_n'-c}{N-1}
\right]_+.
\]
Compactness bounds this price uniformly.
The true own cost is therefore uniformly Lipschitz in allocation
on the relevant deviation set.
If \(D_n=\operatorname{diam}\mathcal X_n\), then
\[
\operatorname{Regret}_{n,\mathcal X_n}
\le
\operatorname{Regret}_{n,\mathcal X_n^\theta}
+K_nD_n\theta.
\]
Indeed, contract an original best-response allocation and keep
its optimizing price.
The cost changes by at most \(K_nD_n\theta\).

For explicit constants, if \(P\) bounds common-price norms and
\(R\) bounds aggregate excess-demand norms, one can take
\[
P_{\mathrm{BR}}=P+\frac R{N-1},
\qquad
K_n=L_n+\frac{\gamma}{N-1}
 (P_{\mathrm{BR}}+NP).
\]
These bounds do not require capping unilateral deviations.
\ledger{40}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 77--95.

The contracted zero is \(\theta a_n\), not zero.
Its same-price tax is at most
\(\lambda^\top\theta a_n\), while
\[
C_n(\theta a_n)-C_n(0)
\le L_n\theta\|a_n\|.
\]
Thus
\[
U_n\ge
-\operatorname{Regret}_{n,\mathcal X_n^\theta}
-\theta(L_n+\|\lambda\|)\|a_n\|.
\]
Participation loses only \(O(\theta)\).

The true optimum contracts to an aggregate-feasible profile:
\[
\sum_n\Phi_n^\theta(x_n^\star)
\le(1-\theta)c+\theta c/2\le c.
\]
Its true cost increases by at most
\(\theta\sum_nL_nD_n\).
Using a common fraction is important; arbitrary separate
fractions need not preserve this inequality.

Applying the preceding saddle transfer on the contracted sets
therefore yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{true regret, participation shortfall,}\\
&\text{positive welfare excess}\\
&\qquad =
O\left(
\mathcal G_T+\theta+
\sum_n\delta_n+\sum_n\Gamma_{n,s_n}^2
\right),\\
&\|r(\bar x)_+\|_1=O(\mathcal G_T),\qquad
\sum_nt_n^c=0.
\end{aligned}
\]
The \(\Gamma\) term is absent for private thresholds.
The expected squared allocation error has the same economic order.
A common sufficient payment restriction is the earlier certificate
together with \(\gamma\le m(N-1)^2/8\).
\ledger{40, 42}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 97--121.

\subsection{A feasible-query boundary example}

The old kink example remains valid for neighborhood queries,
but does not establish a feasible-query lower bound.
For the specific contraction scheme, take
\[
\begin{gathered}
N=3,\quad c=1,\quad \mathcal X_n=[0,2],\\
a_n=r_n=1/6,\qquad
C_n(x)=x+\frac{x^2}{2},\qquad
\delta_n=\theta/12.
\end{gathered}
\]
The contracted lower endpoint is
\(\theta/6=2\delta_n\).
Both empirical smoothing and lifted residual smoothing minimize
there.
For small \(\theta\), capacity is slack and prices are zero.
Each agent's true regret and participation shortfall equal
\[
C_n(\theta/6)-C_n(0)
=\frac{\theta}{6}+\frac{\theta^2}{72}
=\Theta(\theta)=\Theta(\delta_n).
\]
Welfare loss is three times this quantity.
Every query is nonnegative.
This proves a linear contraction-boundary order for the specified
method, not an impossibility for all feasible-query algorithms
or a lower bound attributable solely to smoothing.
\ledger{43}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 123--129.

\section{Appended round: allocation curvature in a uniform gap bound}

Review \ledger{37} observed that the largest noise lies in the
strongly convex allocation block.
The new proof retains that curvature inside the random-comparator
supremum, instead of charging all noise at order \(T^{-1/2}\).
\ledger{41, 42}

Write
\[
\mathcal L(x,v,\lambda)
=H(x,v)+\lambda^\top r(x)
\]
on compact convex product domains \(X,V,\mathcal D\).
The threshold block \(v\) is omitted for empirical batches.
Assume \(H(x,v)-m\|x\|^2/2\) is jointly convex.
The allocation and threshold conditional means form a joint
subgradient; the dual block is exact.
Let
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_t\|\widehat g_{x,t}\|^2&\le B_x^2,
&\mathbb E_t\|\zeta_{x,t}\|^2&\le V_x^2,\\
\mathbb E_t\|\widehat g_{v,t}\|^2&\le B_v^2,
&\mathbb E_t\|\zeta_{v,t}\|^2&\le V_v^2,\\
\|r(x_t)\|&\le B_\lambda.
\end{aligned}
\]
Here \(\zeta\) is oracle minus conditional mean.
One may take \(V_x\le B_x\), \(V_v\le B_v\).
Let domain diameters be \(D_x,D_v,D_\lambda\).

For a fixed horizon, set
\[
w_t=t,\qquad
W_T=\sum_{t=1}^Tt=\frac{T(T+1)}2,\qquad
S_T=\sum_{t=1}^Tt^2.
\]
Use
\[
\begin{aligned}
x_{t+1}
&=\Pi_X\left[
x_t-\frac{4}{m(t+1)}\widehat g_{x,t}
\right],\\
v_{t+1}
&=\Pi_V[v_t-\alpha_vt\widehat g_{v,t}],
&\alpha_v&=\frac{D_v}{B_v\sqrt{S_T}},\\
\lambda_{t+1}
&=\Pi_{\mathcal D}[\lambda_t+\alpha_\lambda t r(x_t)],
&\alpha_\lambda&=\frac{D_\lambda}{B_\lambda\sqrt{S_T}}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Zero-diameter blocks are omitted.
Average all played iterates with weights \(t\).

The new lemma states
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E\mathcal G_T
\le{}&
\frac{4(B_x^2+V_x^2)}{m(T+1)}
+\frac{mD_x^2}{T+1}\\
&+\left[
D_v(B_v+V_v)+D_\lambda B_\lambda
\right]\frac{\sqrt{S_T}}{W_T}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Only the allocation second moment is charged at order \(1/T\).
Flat, nonsmooth threshold directions are permitted.
\ledger{42}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 131--174.

\subsection{Projection telescoping with reserved curvature}

For any comparator,
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\mathcal L(x_t,v_t,\lambda)-\mathcal L(x,v,\lambda_t)\\
&\quad\le
\langle g_{x,t},x_t-x\rangle
+\langle g_{v,t},v_t-v\rangle\\
&\qquad
-\langle r(x_t),\lambda_t-\lambda\rangle
-\frac m2\|x_t-x\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
Reserve half the allocation curvature.
With \(\eta_{x,t}=4/[m(t+1)]\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\sum_{t=1}^Tt
\left[
\langle\widehat g_{x,t},x_t-x\rangle
-\frac m4\|x_t-x\|^2
\right]\\
&\qquad\le
\frac2m\sum_{t=1}^T
 \frac{t}{t+1}\|\widehat g_{x,t}\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
The cancellation is
\[
\frac{t}{2\eta_{x,t}}
-\frac{t-1}{2\eta_{x,t-1}}
=\frac{mt}{4}.
\]
The first distance coefficient cancels as well, and the final
distance term is nonpositive.
Expected allocation contribution is at most \(2TB_x^2/m\).

For a flat block with step \(\alpha t\), weighted projection regret
is at most
\[
\frac{D^2}{2\alpha}
+\frac\alpha2\sum_{t=1}^Tt^2\|\widehat g_t\|^2.
\]
The stated choice of \(\alpha\) bounds its expectation by
\(DB\sqrt{S_T}\).
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 176--207.
\ledger{42}

\subsection{The allocation martingale supremum}

The remaining allocation term is
\[
\sup_{x\in X}\sum_{t=1}^Tt f_t(x),
\qquad
f_t(x)=
\langle\zeta_{x,t},x-x_t\rangle
-\frac m4\|x-x_t\|^2.
\]
A proof-only auxiliary sequence starts in \(X\) and follows
\[
\begin{aligned}
u_{t+1}
&=\Pi_X\left[
u_t+\frac{t}{\sigma W_t}
 \{\zeta_{x,t}-\sigma(u_t-x_t)\}
\right],\\
\sigma&=m/2.
\end{aligned}
\]
It need not be computed by the learner.
The point \(u_t\) is measurable before the current noise.

Weighted strong concavity and projection give, pathwise,
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\sup_{x\in X}
 \sum_{t=1}^Tt[f_t(x)-f_t(u_t)]\\
&\qquad\le
\sum_{t=1}^T\frac{t^2}{2\sigma W_t}
 \|\zeta_{x,t}-\sigma(u_t-x_t)\|^2.
\end{aligned}
\]
The coefficient cancellation uses
\(\sigma W_t-\sigma W_{t-1}=\sigma t\).
Conditional centering gives
\[
\mathbb E_t f_t(u_t)
=-\frac m4\|u_t-x_t\|^2\le0
\]
and
\[
\mathbb E_t
 \|\zeta_{x,t}-\sigma(u_t-x_t)\|^2
\le V_x^2+\frac{m^2D_x^2}{4}.
\]
Since
\[
\frac{t^2}{2\sigma W_t}
=\frac{2t}{m(t+1)}\le\frac2m,
\]
it follows that
\[
\mathbb E\sup_{x\in X}\sum_{t=1}^Tt f_t(x)
\le
\frac{2T}{m}
\left(V_x^2+\frac{m^2D_x^2}{4}\right).
\]

The threshold martingale has no curvature and is bounded by
\(D_vV_v\sqrt{S_T}\) using orthogonality.
There is no dual-oracle noise.
Adding the terms, dividing by \(W_T\), and using
convexity-concavity of the weighted averages proves the lemma.
The argument needs conditional second moments, not independence
across blocks or differentiability at threshold atoms.
\ledger{42}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 209--248.

\section{Appended round: the corrected total-query comparison}

Fix dimensions, geometry, positive tail masses, curvature,
public bounds, cap margin, and payment scale.
Choose
\[
\theta\asymp\epsilon,\qquad
\delta_n\asymp\epsilon,\qquad
\delta_n\le\theta r_n/2.
\]
Constants cannot be suppressed uniformly if geometry, curvature,
or tail masses deteriorate with accuracy.

For private thresholds,
\(B_x^2=O(\delta_{\min}^{-2})\), while threshold and dual
bounds remain constant.
Therefore
\[
\mathbb E\mathcal G_T
=O\left(
\frac1{\delta_{\min}^2T}+\frac1{\sqrt T}
\right).
\]
Taking \(T=O(\epsilon^{-3})\) gives expected
\(O(\epsilon)\) true regret, participation shortfall,
capacity violation, positive welfare excess, and squared allocation
error.
Budget balance is exact.
There are two queries per active agent per update.

For symmetric empirical batches,
\[
B_x^2=O\left(1+
 \frac1{s_{\min}\delta_{\min}^2}\right),
\]
and there is no threshold block.
Taking \(s_n\asymp\epsilon^{-1}\) and
\(T=O(\epsilon^{-2})\) gives the same economic accuracy.
Each update costs \(O(\epsilon^{-1})\) queries for fixed \(N\).
Thus both methods have total-query upper bound
\[
O(\epsilon^{-3}).
\]
Thresholds use more updates with constant query work;
symmetric batches use fewer updates with larger batches.
Neither is declared universally better.
\ledger{42, 45}
See \url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 250--281.

For completeness, applying the same weighted learner to the older
oracles gives the retained comparisons
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{Unbaselined empirical batch}
 &O(\epsilon^{-4}),\\
\text{Independent symmetric batches}
 &O(\epsilon^{-3}),\\
\text{Multilevel one-point values}
 &O(\epsilon^{-3}\log^2(1/\epsilon)),\\
\text{Private sphere/ball thresholds}
 &O(\epsilon^{-3}).
\end{array}
\]
The multilevel bound is expected work.
These are upper bounds for specified algorithms, not minimax
rates or demonstrated practical speedups.
All preferred-method queries can be locally feasible, without
common random numbers.

