\section{What remains open and the smallest next step}

The retained mechanism result directly implements efficient
allocation with budget balance and participation under its stated
assumptions.
Q's incompatible strict LMIs, unique-message claim, and original
participation claim are not retained.
The empirical-CVaR analysis gives atom-safe Lipschitz bias and
order-tight \(O(\delta+s^{-1})\) economic distortion within the
specified surrogate class.
Review \ledger{37} supports those derivations.
\ledger{2, 5, 16, 29, 30, 37}

The appended round resolves the stated local-query objection by
relative-interior contraction, with an \(O(\theta)\) economic cost
and a uniform multiplier cap.
It corrects the fair baseline and supplies a conditional
\(O(\epsilon^{-3})\) total-query upper bound for both private
thresholds and independent symmetric batches.
Outputs are weighted averages with common prices.
Exact budget balance holds there, but aggregate capacity
feasibility is only approximate at finite time.
\ledger{39, 40, 42, 45}

The new proof evidence is still thin in specific respects:
there is no later independent audit, no convergence experiment for
these learners, and no complete priority comparison.
Explicit dependence on dimension, tail mass, geometry, and cap
margin should be collected before a paper-level complexity claim.
Optimal oracle complexity, zero-capacity resources, exact
finite-time aggregate feasibility, and incentives to follow the
learning protocol remain open.
Nonunique prices prevent an unrestricted full-message convergence
claim.
\ledger{44, 45}

The smallest next mathematical step is a focused independent audit
of the newest uniform-gap and feasible-query arguments:
\url{ada/feasible_queries_and_curvature.md}, lines 18--29,
63--121, and 178--248.
The decisive checks are the independent-batch variance calculation,
the anchor-based uniform cap, contraction of unrestricted
best-response deviations, and the curvature-penalized random
comparator supremum.
If these hold, the query counts follow directly from lines
250--281.
This is a narrower task than reopening the already reviewed
mechanism, bias, or earlier saddle-transfer proofs.

The separate publication-priority task is full-text comparison
against Hurwicz, Chen, Koshal, and the broader price-consensus
mechanism literature.
The pool misses do not establish absence of prior work.
The ledger recommends proceeding toward a draft while preserving
these limitations, rather than claiming novelty for generic
CVaR lifting, multilevel estimation, weighted averaging, or
regularization.
\ledger{44, 45, 46}

This response preserves the preceding mathematical results and
appends the latest bounded round, with the earlier baseline
interpretation corrected.
The document was not compiled in this turn:
\texttt{pdflatex} was unavailable, and local access restrictions
blocked the automated style tools.
No compilation or automated style-gate pass is claimed.

